Q: Category Import in Magento without products A: <p>Please refer my tutorial which create category and subcategory using the magento script.</p>

<p><a href="http://www.pearlbells.co.uk/import-the-categories-programmatically/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://www.pearlbells.co.uk/import-the-categories-programmatically/</a></p>

<pre><code>foreach ($arrResult as $import_category) {
    try {
        if (strtolower($import_category[16]) == 'true') {
            $enabled = 1;
        } else {
            $enabled = 0;
        }

        if ($import_category[1] == 0) {
            $parentId = '2';
        }
        else {
            $parentId = $list[$import_category[1]];
        }

        $category = Mage::getModel('catalog/category');
        $category-&gt;setName($import_category[2]);
        $category-&gt;setMetaTitle($import_category[2]);
        $category-&gt;setIncludeInMenu(1);
        $category-&gt;setUrlKey($import_category[10]);
        $category-&gt;setDescription(strip_tags($import_category[11]));
        $category-&gt;setMetaDescription($import_category[12]);
        $category-&gt;setMetaKeywords($import_category[13]);
        $category-&gt;setIsActive($enabled);
        $category-&gt;setDisplayMode('PRODUCTS');
        $category-&gt;setIsAnchor(1); //for active anchor
        $category-&gt;setStoreId(Mage::app()-&gt;getStore()-&gt;getId());
        $parentCategory = Mage::getModel('catalog/category')-&gt;load($parentId);
        $category-&gt;setPath($parentCategory-&gt;getPath());
        $category-&gt;setCustomUseParentSettings(true);
        $category-&gt;setImage($import_category[6]);
        $category-&gt;save();
        $list[$import_category[0]] = $category-&gt;getId();
        echo 'Category ' . $category-&gt;getName() . ' ' . $category-&gt;getId() . ' imported successfully' . PHP_EOL;
    } catch (Exception $e) {
        echo 'Something failed for category ' . $import_category[2] . PHP_EOL;
        print_r($e);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Spring Security configuration: HTTP 403 error A: <p>In my case, the user didn't exist and a RuntimeException is thrown. I leaved like that and understood spring security handles it that way.</p>
<p>I had to debug inside spring classed to find out.</p>
<pre><code>@Service
public class UserDetailsServiceImpl implements UserDetailsService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) throws UsernameNotFoundException {

        Supplier&lt;RuntimeException&gt; lambda = () -&gt; new RuntimeException(&quot;User not found&quot;);
        User user = userRepository.findByEmail(username).orElseThrow(lambda);
        return new UserDetailsImpl(user);

    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Check whether list of custom objects have same value for a property in Java 8 A: <p>This is a somewhat verbose solution (unless you're reusing the <code>Gatherer</code> in different places in your code), but in Java 24 or later you could use a custom <code>Gatherer</code> to do this.</p>
<pre><code>class State {
    String value;
    boolean allSame;
}
boolean allSame = list.stream().gather(Gatherer.of(
        State::new,
        (State state, A element, Gatherer.Downstream&lt;? super Boolean&gt; downstream) -&gt; {
            if (state.value == null || state.value.equals(element.getName())) {
                state.value = element.getName();
                return true;
            } else {
                state.allSame = true;
                return false;
            }
        },
        (s1, s2) -&gt; {
            s1.value = s1.value == null ? s2.value : s1.value;
            s1.allSame = s1.allSame || s2.allSame ||
                    (s1.value != null &amp;&amp; s2.value != null &amp;&amp; s1.value.equals(s2.value));

            return s1;
        },
        (state1, downstream1) -&gt;
                downstream1.push(state1.allSame))).findFirst().orElseThrow();
</code></pre>
<p>This <code>Gatherer</code> converts the <code>Stream&lt;A&gt;</code> into a single-element <code>Stream&lt;Boolean&gt;</code> indicating whether there were any differing elements.  It keeps tracks of elements encountered in a custom <code>State</code> type, and short-circuits with a <code>false</code> if an element is encountered that didn't match the others.</p>
<p>I haven't properly microbenchmarked it, but some quick testing locally finds it to be faster than the <code>distinct().limit(2)</code> approach for a sequential stream over a very large <code>List</code>, and similar in speed for a parallel stream over the <code>List</code>.  Though for most practical purposes, I don't expect that to matter.</p>
